00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.160
So then what is our conclusion here?

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:05.720
To say that Star Wars outright ripped off Valerian ignores all the innovative things unique to Star Wars.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:11.000
The Force, lightsabers, Death Star, wookiees, ewoks, Yoda, and on and on and on.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:13.820
Sure, the parallels don't make Star Wars look all that great,

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:18.100
but it's not as though it copied Valerian word-for-word, concept-for-concept.

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:20.340
It borrowed elements from other sources too.

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:24.740
Remember when Luke returns home in A New Hope to find his Aunt and Uncle's farm has been torched?

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:30.860
Yeah, well Lucas has gone on record to say that that was inspired by a John Wayne movie called The Searchers from 1956.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:39.380
The attack on the Death Star at the end of A New Hope is even less ambiguously borrowed from a 1955 British World War II film called The Dam Busters.

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:43.980
Instead of an X-Wing squadron going into a trench to fire on the Death Star's single weak spot,

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:50.860
The Dam Busters has a squadron of fighter pilots flying low against a river bed to fire on a single weak spot of the Dam.

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:56.060
And while that may not sound too similar on paper, if there's any question about whether one influenced the other,

00:09:56.060 --> 00:10:03.280
a side-by-side comparison shows that Lucas basically recreates the entire sequence shot-for-shot.

